 by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIf $\\log_{x-1}(10-2x)=2$, find $x$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"0e52a7b4ed9f28a1bcc46650662920082e2710f48e3549f9f2a830704b018a53"},"seconds":11.698818683624268,"seed":285283436888269056,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation \\(\\log_{x-1}(10-2x) = 2\\), we need to convert the logarithmic equation into its exponential form. The equation \\(\\log_{x-1}(10-2x) = 2\\) can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n(x-1)^2 = 10 - 2x\n\\]\n\nNext, we expand and simplify the equation:\n\n\\[\n(x-1)^2 = x^2 - 2x + 1\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 - 2x + 1 = 10 - 2x\n\\]\n\nWe can eliminate the \\(-2x\\) term from both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 1 = 10\n\\]\n\nSubtract 1 from both sides:\n\n\\[\nx^2 = 9\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\nx = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -3\n\\]\n\nHowever, we need to check if these solutions satisfy the original logarithmic equation. The base of the logarithm, \\(x-1\\), must be positive and not equal to 1. Therefore, \\(x-1 > 0\\) and \\(x-1 \\neq 1\\), which means \\(x > 1\\) and \\(x \\neq 2\\).\n\nFor \\(x = 3\\):\n\n\\[\nx-1 = 3-1 = 2 \\quad \\text{(which is valid since } 2 > 1 \\text{ and } 2 \\neq 1\\text{)}\n\\]\n\\[\n10 - 2x = 10 - 2(3) = 4 \\quad \\text{(which is positive)}\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(x = 3\\) is a valid solution.\n\nFor \\(x = -3\\):\n\n\\[\nx-1 = -3-1 = -4 \\quad \\text{(which is not valid since } -4 \\leq 1\\text{)}\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(x = -3\\) is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only valid solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{3}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:432"}
